<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.5.1: Reviewing Zero Product Property</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02159</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.5.1: Reviewing Zero Product Property</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5727756e-3bea-4c33-b339-2186fa277b47</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Decide whether each statement is true or false.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="f89d3e28-cd75-4b37-b50b-01146a52c286" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6992756e-5681-457f-b6a7-c2e9550f74e2" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="False" data-solution-options="[&quot;True&quot;, &quot;False&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>3 is the only solution to \(x^2−9=0\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! False. There are two solutions because there are two numbers that can be squared to get 9. Both 3 and –3 are possible solutions.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="4f56e224-75d6-447b-8675-412ccf6c4c52" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="False" data-solution-options="[&quot;True&quot;, &quot;False&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A solution to \(x^2+25=0\) is –5. </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! False. There is no solution to \(x^2+25=0\) because no real number can be squared to get –25.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a84ff741-2f65-4dcd-a5eb-bc0584d6f53a" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="True" data-solution-options="[&quot;True&quot;, &quot;False&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>\(x(x−7)=0\) has two solutions.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! True. There are two factors, 0 and 7, that could make the product of the expression 0.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e4012223-6761-4182-9aa8-3f81e734d6b5" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="False" data-solution-options="[&quot;True&quot;, &quot;False&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>5 and –7 are the solutions to \((x−5)(x+7)=12\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! False. The product of the two factors is not 0, so we cannot solve it using the zero product property (which gives 5 and –7 as solutions). If we evaluate the expression \((x−5)(x+7)\) at those two values, the result will be 0, not 12.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
